# Fixtures, Mocks, and Determinism

Tests that depend on actual time, randomness, sleeping, or network calls can fail for reasons unrelated to the code under test. Control all four.

## Mock Table Methods and Container Services

Mock a table method with `getMockForModel()` — it avoids the reflected-property issues plain mocks have with tables. Clear the mock in `tearDown()` so later tests get the real table:

```php
public function testSendingEmails(): void
{
    $model = $this->getMockForModel('EmailVerification', ['send']);
    $model->expects($this->once())
        ->method('send')
        ->will($this->returnValue(true));

    $model->verifyEmail('test@example.com');
}

public function tearDown(): void
{
    parent::tearDown();
    $this->getTableLocator()->clear();
}
```

Prefer `mockModel()` when the project uses Mockery doubles. Replace a container-injected service with `$this->mockService(StripeService::class, function () { ... })` — the mock is injected into controllers and commands under test; undo with `$this->removeMockService(StripeService::class)`.

Use a mock only when the real implementation leaves the process or is nondeterministic. Use the real implementation for everything else, including the database.

## Outbound HTTP

Mock HTTP with `HttpClientTrait` so no test reaches the network. Fake the exact endpoint used by each test — a fake without an endpoint accepts unexpected requests and can hide defects:

```php
use Cake\Http\TestSuite\HttpClientTrait;

class CartControllerTest extends TestCase
{
    use HttpClientTrait;

    public function testCheckout(): void
    {
        $this->mockClientPost(
            'https://example.com/process-payment',
            $this->newClientResponse(200, ['Content-Type: application/json'], json_encode(['ok' => true])),
        );
        $this->post('/cart/checkout');
        $this->assertResponseSuccess();
    }
}
```

## Events and Mail

Assert dispatched events instead of re-testing their listeners' results. Enable tracking on the manager under test, then assert the event and its payload:

```php
$this->Orders->getEventManager()->setEventList(new EventList());
$this->assertTrue($this->Orders->place($order));
$this->assertEventFired('Model.Order.afterPlace', $this->Orders->getEventManager());
$this->assertEventFiredWith('Model.Order.afterPlace', 'order', $order, $this->Orders->getEventManager());
```

The global `EventManager` is the default for assertions, so global events need no manager argument. Assert sent mail through the mailer's test transport and captured log output through log testing — one assertion per double, stating what was dispatched or that nothing was.

{% if pest %}
- Create each double inside the test that needs it. Do not create doubles in a file-level `beforeEach()`.
{% else %}
- Create each double inside the test method that needs it. Do not create doubles in `setUp()`.
{% endif %}

## Time and Randomness

- Freeze or travel the time in each test that depends on a date, a period, or a timestamp, using the time helper the project already uses.
- Fix generated values (identifiers, slugs) when the test asserts them, instead of asserting randomness.
- Never call a real sleep in a test. Restore time and randomness after each test, if the suite does not restore them for every test.

## Database

- Run real queries against real fixture rows in the test database. Do not mock the query builder, because the test then asserts the mock.
- Declare every fixture a test will query via `$fixtures` or `getFixtures()`. Prefer `TransactionStrategy` when tests do not depend on resetting auto-increment between methods.
- Test application behavior caused by the schema, such as deleting dependent records through a cascade. Do not test the database engine's cascade implementation.
